<article class="env-lemma" id="0END">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0END">Lemma <span data-tag="0END">72.4.2</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an integral algebraic space over $S$. Let $\eta \in |X|$ be the generic point of $X$. There are canonical identifications </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R(X) = \mathcal{O}_{X, \eta }^ h = \kappa (\eta )  \]
</div>
  <p> where $R(X)$ is the ring of rational functions defined in Morphisms of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/0EMP" data-tag="0EMP">67.47.3</a>, $\kappa (\eta )$ is the residue field defined in Decent Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/0EMW" data-tag="0EMW">68.11.2</a>, and $\mathcal{O}_{X, \eta }^ h$ is the henselian local ring defined in Decent Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/0BGU" data-tag="0BGU">68.11.5</a>. In particular, these rings are fields. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $X$ is a scheme in an open neighbourhood of $\eta $ (see discussion above), this follows immediately from the corresponding result for schemes, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01RV" data-tag="01RV">29.50.5</a>. We also use: the henselianization of a field is itself and that our definitions of these objects for algebraic spaces are compatible with those for schemes. Details omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>